INSTITUTIONAL FRAMEWORK
Article 79
(1) The Women's Empowerment and Child Protection System is the entire process of implementing women's empowerment and child protection conducted comprehensively, inclusively and integratively starting from the stage of report/complaint handling services, health services, social rehabilitation, repatriation, social reintegration and legal assistance up to repatriation and social reintegration for women and children victims of crime and violence in their social environment.
(2) The Women's Empowerment and Child Protection System aims to:
a. improve the quality of life of women, children and family quality;
b. increase the institutional capacity of women's empowerment and child protection including the development of gender and child data; and
c. provide special protection for children from various forms of violence and other discriminatory treatment.
(3) The Women's Empowerment and Child Protection System is implemented through:
a. providing access for women and children to education services, health and other strategic fields;
b. encouraging the involvement of women and children in the development process;
c. providing knowledge, skills, character values, morals and family resilience;
d. encouraging programs that can increase women's independence in the economic, political, legal, socio-cultural fields as well as other strategic fields;
e. establishment of development and strengthening of the capacity of Women and Child Protection institutions including complaint service units for violence against women and children as well as legal aid services;
f. improving the quality of human resources of managers;
g. strengthening the institutional capacity of regional gender and child mainstreaming;
h. strengthening and developing gender and child data systems; and
i. conducting promotive, preventive, curative and rehabilitative efforts in accordance with Laws and Regulations.
(4) In the context of comprehensive, inclusive and integrative women's empowerment and child protection in the region, the Regional Government establishes:
a. P2TP2A or similar institutions that have the same functions and roles;
b. regional child protection commission or other similar institutions to support supervision of the implementation of child protection in the region; and
c. other institutions according to needs and/or provisions of Laws and Regulations.

Article 20
The implementation of protection for children and women is carried out through:
a. planning and development of information systems for children and women;
b. prevention and protection;
c. child protection;
d. special protection;
e. protection of women;
f. protection in the workplace;
g. handling and recovery of victims of violence;
h. rehabilitation, reintegration, and empowerment;
i. law enforcement services and legal assistance; and
j. facilitation of juvenile justice.

Article 64
(1) The provisions as referred to in Article 63 paragraph (1), may be exempted by taking into account the following requirements: a. age for child workers in informal sector work above 15 (fifteen) years;
b. obtain written consent from parents/guardians of child workers in informal sector work;
c. implementation must be set forth in a written work agreement between the company/employer and the parents/guardians of child workers in informal sector work;
d. not employed at night;
e. working time of at most 3 (three) hours per day;
f. not employed in a place or environment that can disrupt the child's growth and development process, whether physical, mental, moral and intellectual or the child's health;
g. provide opportunities to socialize with family and the surrounding environment;
h. provide opportunities to obtain education in accordance with their talents and interests;
t. employed for light types of work; and
J. provide health protection for children and the opportunity for one day off per week.
(2) Protection for child workers in informal sector work as referred to in paragraph (1), aims to: a. prevent all forms of exploitation, discrimination, harassment and violence against children; and
b. protect children from activities that can disrupt the child's growth and development process, whether physical, mental, moral and intellectual or the child's health.
(3) Protection efforts for child workers in informal sector work as referred to in paragraph (2), include among others in the form of: a. providing education to the community about children's rights; b. providing assistance in the form of psychological, medical and legal services to child workers in informal sector work who experience exploitation, discrimination, harassment and violence; c. empowering families through the provision of skills training and expenditure reduction;
d. providing scholarships to child workers in informal sector work who have dropped out of school to continue formal education to a higher level; and e. providing non-formal education and skills training for child workers in informal sector work who do not pursue formal education.

